A||||4||ChadMan||Question for you motorcyclists||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||04-04-2001||09:00 AM||||I know we have some bikers on here so I thought I'd ask this question.  How hard is it to replace fork seals?  I have leaky ones on my '82 Yamaha Maxim 750.  The shop wants $140 to replace them.  I'm thinking of doing it myself, but have never done any bike suspension work.<P>Should I do this myself, or have the shop do it?  Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||04-04-2001||11:08 AM||||itns't not that difficult but their are springs in the shock you'll have to contend with. you'll need a heavy LOONG person to press the springs back in... I'd say it'd be worth the $ for somone else to have the aggrivation. After fighting with a 93 FZR that wouldn't start for 3 days i figured out it had a bad swtich.. well after calling the dealer there was a recall (out of date/service like our Fieros/recalls) but he "could have told me right off the bat.." yeah whatever old man... still .. $140 isn't that bad... do they offer any kinda warranty? 30 or 60 day? <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||04-04-2001||01:09 PM||||It's not too hard a job if you've got a good manual.  On a bike that old, make sure the sliders(shiny part of fork) aren't worn or new seals will start to leak again pretty soon.  An experienced mech will know what other parts to check for wear and be able to tell if they need replacement.  That sounds like a fair price.  I would pay the bucks unless you just want the experience of learning about the fork innards by doing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||04-04-2001||02:05 PM||||My 82 Honda GL500 needs them. I plan to do it myself 'when I get around to it'. Looking at the manual it looks like the hardest part will be putting the springs back in like 1fst2m6 mentioned. I've done that part of the job before on mine when I replaced the fork oil. Definitely not the easiest thing to do... but not impossible either (at least on my bike). There really aren't a lot of parts involved here. The only thing I can think of that hasn't been mentioned is; be sure to check the sliders for straightness while you have it apart.<P>$140 is not that bad for the work involved... doing it yourself with a manual is not unreasonable either... Really comes down to personal preference; free time vs. extra cash, new adventure vs. aggrevation, 'did I miss something' vs. 'did THEY miss something', etc...<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||04-04-2001||02:11 PM||||If you've got a shop manual, it's raining outside, and you've got a long afternoon to kill its not hard to do at all. I've done it on a couple of bikes.<P>If the weather is beautiful and you've got an awesome daytrip planned out have the garage do it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
